[%# The syslog listener for the fleet.  Provisioner::Recipe::logcollector gives
    the full reasons.  Two facts matter here.

    The input has its own ruleset.  So a message from the network is written once,
    to the file for its sender, and never gets to the default rules.  The stop
    below applies only to remote messages.  Without the ruleset, it discards the
    local logs of this guest too.

    secpath-replace is necessary.  HOSTNAME is the name that the sender put in the
    message.  Without secpath-replace, a sender can call itself
    ../../etc/cron.d/oops and choose where this guest writes. -%]
[% IF protocol == 'tcp' || protocol == 'both' -%]
module(load="imtcp")
input(type="imtcp" port="[% port %]" ruleset="logcollector")
[% END -%]
[% IF protocol == 'udp' || protocol == 'both' -%]
module(load="imudp")
input(type="imudp" port="[% port %]" ruleset="logcollector")
[% END -%]
[% FOR sender IN allowed_senders -%]
$AllowedSender TCP, [% sender %]
$AllowedSender UDP, [% sender %]
[% END -%]

template(name="logcollector-perhost" type="string" string="[% log_dir %]/%HOSTNAME:::secpath-replace%.log")

ruleset(name="logcollector") {
    action(type="omfile" dynaFile="logcollector-perhost" dirCreateMode="0755" fileCreateMode="0640")
[%# Above the stop, because stop ends the processing of the message.  A forward
    below it never runs.

    RFC5424 with octet-counted framing, because the telegraf syslog input parses
    that.  It rejects RFC3164, the default format of rsyslog.  These copies go to
    a consumer on this guest (see Provisioner::Recipe::logcollector). -%]
[% FOR dest IN forward -%]
[% parts = dest.split(':') -%]
    action(type="omfwd" target="[% parts.0 %]" port="[% parts.1 %]" protocol="tcp"
           template="RSYSLOG_SyslogProtocol23Format" TCP_Framing="octet-counted"
           action.resumeRetryCount="-1"
           queue.type="linkedList" queue.size="10000")
[% END -%]
    stop
}
